---
/**
 * How the content was made, in public.
 *
 * A site that teaches people to ask where an AI answer came from cannot be
 * coy about where its own pages came from. So the numbers on this page are
 * computed from the corpus at build time rather than written by hand: if the
 * review backlog grows, this page says so without anyone editing it.
 */
import Learn from '../../layouts/Learn.astro';
import { TERMS, CORPUS_STATS, learnHref, siteHref } from '../../lib/learn/terms';

const models = [...new Set(TERMS.map((t) => t.generation?.model).filter(Boolean))] as string[];
const reviewers = [...new Set(TERMS.map((t) => t.machineReview?.deployment).filter(Boolean))] as string[];
const machineReviewed = TERMS.filter((t) => t.machineReview).length;
const rewritten = TERMS.filter((t) => (t.machineReview?.applied.length ?? 0) > 0).length;
const openErrors = TERMS.flatMap((t) => t.machineReview?.issues ?? []).filter(
  (i) => i.severity === 'error',
).length;
const promptVersions = [...new Set(TERMS.map((t) => t.generation?.promptVersion).filter(Boolean))] as string[];
const withCaution = TERMS.filter((t) => t.unWorkplaceExample.caution).length;
const withContested = TERMS.filter((t) => t.contested).length;
const resources = TERMS.flatMap((t) => t.resources);
const verified = resources.filter((r) => r.verified).length;
const searches = resources.filter((r) => r.isSearch).length;

const title = 'Methodology · UNAIVERSE';
const description =
  'How the AI Literacy Universe was written: an offline Azure OpenAI pipeline, a strict schema, an automated link checker, and a second model that reviews the first one\'s work.';
---

<Learn title={title} description={description} section="methodology">
  <header class="learn-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="learn-crumbs">
        <a href={learnHref()}>Learn AI</a> / Methodology
      </p>
      <h1 class="learn-head__title">How these pages were written</h1>
      <p class="learn-head__lead">
        This site tells you to ask where an AI answer came from. It would be a poor advertisement
        for that advice if it did not answer the question about itself.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="wrap" style="padding-block: var(--sp-6); max-width: 46rem">
    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="short">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="short">The short version</h2>
      <p>
        Each term was drafted by a large language model running through a script on a developer's
        machine, against a fixed prompt and a strict schema. The draft was checked automatically for
        structure, for required fields, for valid links to other terms, and for reading level. Every
        external URL was fetched to see whether it answers. A second, different model then reviewed
        the first model's work, looking for what was wrong with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Being precise about what that does and does not mean.</strong> A model checking a
        model is a real check and a limited one. It catches arithmetic that does not compute,
        definitions that fail to separate themselves from neighbouring terms, quiz answers that are
        not defensible, and examples that present invented specifics as real. It does not reliably
        catch a confident, fluent, wrong account of how something works. So: use these pages to get
        oriented, and check anything that carries weight against the primary sources every page
        links to.
      </p>
      <p>
        The published site is a folder of static files on GitHub Pages. It makes no call to any AI
        service, holds no credential, and could not reach one if it tried.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="numbers">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="numbers">Where the corpus stands</h2>
      <ul class="az-list" style="grid-template-columns: 1fr">
        <li><a href={learnHref('explore')}><span>Terms published</span><span class="az-list__meta">{CORPUS_STATS.published} of {CORPUS_STATS.planned} planned</span></a></li>
        <li><a href={learnHref('explore')}><span>Checked by a second model</span><span class="az-list__meta">{machineReviewed}</span></a></li>
        <li><a href={learnHref('map')}><span>Categories</span><span class="az-list__meta">{CORPUS_STATS.categories}</span></a></li>
        <li><a href={learnHref('explore')}><span>Hand-built interactive explainers</span><span class="az-list__meta">{CORPUS_STATS.withInteractive}</span></a></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="learn-head__meta">
        {withCaution} terms carry an explicit caution about confidential or personal data.{' '}
        {withContested} are flagged as contested terminology. {verified} of {resources.length}{' '}
        external links have been fetched and answered; {searches} are deliberately search links
        rather than a single page.

      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="pipeline">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="pipeline">The pipeline, step by step</h2>
      <ol class="steps-list">
        <li><span><strong>A taxonomy file defines the vocabulary.</strong> {CORPUS_STATS.planned} terms across {CORPUS_STATS.categories} categories, each with a difficulty, an audience, its prerequisites and the terms it is commonly confused with. Written by hand.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Resource links are assembled before generation, never by the model.</strong> They come from a hand-authored list, from the Wikipedia API (which either returns a real article or does not), or from a constructed search URL. The model is shown the links and writes only the sentence explaining why each is useful. It is never asked for a URL, because a model asked for a URL will eventually invent one.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>One term at a time goes to Azure OpenAI</strong>{models.length ? ` (${models.join(', ')})` : ''} with a fixed system prompt and a JSON Schema the response must match.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>The answer is validated against the same schema the site uses.</strong> A draft missing its analogy limitation, its screen-reader description or a defensible quiz answer is rejected and regenerated with the specific failures fed back to the model. Rejected attempts are kept on disk.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Every URL is fetched.</strong> Links that answer are marked as checked with a date. Links that do not are labelled "not verified" on the page rather than quietly presented as vetted.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>A quality report is generated:</strong> reading-level outliers, duplicate definitions, orphan terms, prerequisites pointing at unwritten pages, missing UN examples, broken links.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>A second, different model reviews the first one's work.</strong>{reviewers.length ? ` Currently ${reviewers.join(', ')}, ` : ' '}prompted to find errors rather than to approve: arithmetic that does not compute, definitions that fail to distinguish themselves from neighbouring terms, quiz answers that are not defensible, anthropomorphism, and examples that present invented specifics as real. It may rewrite a bounded set of fields, never introduce a new fact or URL, and every rewrite is re-validated against the same schema before it is kept.{rewritten ? ` ${rewritten} pages were rewritten as a result.` : ''}</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Publishing is a separate, deliberate step.</strong> The site renders one directory, and a term is in it only because someone ran the command that put it there.</span></li>
      </ol>

      <p class="data-note" style="margin-top: var(--sp-4)">
        The second-model pass raised {openErrors} observation{openErrors === 1 ? '' : 's'} at error
        level across the corpus, and rewrote {rewritten} pages as a result. The rest are recorded in
        the repository rather than on the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="rules">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="rules">The writing rules</h2>
      <ul class="steps-list">
        <li><span>Define the term immediately. Short sentences, one new idea at a time, no explaining jargon with more jargon.</span></li>
        <li><span>Every analogy states where it stops working. This is enforced by the schema, not by good intentions. A term without it cannot be published.</span></li>
        <li><span>No anthropomorphism. A model does not understand, think, know, want or decide, and the validator flags drafts that say it does.</span></li>
        <li><span>No hype and no doom. Fluent output is never treated as evidence of accuracy.</span></li>
        <li><span>Examples come from many regions and many kinds of work. No cats, no dogs, no recipes.</span></li>
        <li><span>United Nations examples never suggest putting confidential, personal, survivor, witness or security-sensitive information into an unapproved public AI service. Member States and contested political questions are treated neutrally.</span></li>
        <li><span>Where terminology is genuinely disputed, the page says so instead of picking a side.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="limits">What this means you should not assume</h2>
      <p>
        These pages are a starting point, not an authority. They were drafted by a model and checked
        by another model, which catches a great deal but not everything. Where a definition matters, in negotiated
        text, in a procurement document, in a legal opinion, go to the primary source, which every
        page links to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Terminology in this field moves. A page written today can be describing yesterday's
        consensus. Each term records the prompt version it was written under{promptVersions.length ? ` (currently ${promptVersions.join(', ')})` : ''} so that a
        regeneration pass can find the stale ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is an independent educational project by one person. It is not a United Nations
        product, it is not endorsed by the United Nations, and nothing in it represents the views of
        the Organization or any of its offices.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="privacy">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No login, no account, no password, no user database. No analytics, no cookies, no tracking
        pixels, no third-party scripts, no fonts loaded from anyone else's server. What you search
        for, what you save and what you get wrong in a quick check stay in your browser and are
        never transmitted, including to the AI service that helped write these pages, which is not
        connected to the published site in any way.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href={learnHref('saved')}>Everything stored locally, and a button to delete it</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="lsection" aria-labelledby="source">
      <h2 class="lsection__title" id="source">See for yourself</h2>
      <p>
        The taxonomy, the prompts, the schema, the generation scripts, the validator, the link
        checker and every published term file are in the repository.
      </p>
      <p class="chips">
        <a class="chip" href="https://github.com/MafiAtUN/unaiverse" rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
        <a class="chip" href={siteHref()}>The UN AI timeline</a>
        <a class="chip" href={learnHref()}>Back to Learn AI</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Learn>
